# Linux auditd records forwarded to syslog by the audisp-syslog plugin (ident
# audisp-syslog / audispd / audit). Two banning rules for remote authentication
# failures and the audit subsystem's own login-failure anomaly, plus a broad
# detection-only sweep of the shapes a compromise leaves in the audit trail:
# account/group management, SELinux AVC denials and AppArmor denials; a NIC
# going promiscuous; mandatory access control switched off, by setenforce or
# by profile removal; the audit subsystem tampered with; a process ending on a
# fault signal or an execution the kernel forbade; a kernel module load; the
# dynamic-linker preload config touched; an exec out of a writable directory;
# and paths named to hide. For a watcher tailing the forwarded audit stream.
# Reference as %syslog/linux-audit%.
#
# NOT a member: syslog/linux-apparmor-denied. It reads the same AppArmor
# denials off the kernel ring buffer (PROGRAM=kernel) for hosts with no auditd
# at all, so it will never see a line on this stream. Name it on whatever
# watcher tails the kernel facility.
#
# See docs/rules.md (Rule groups).

syslog/linux-auditd-abend
syslog/linux-auditd-anom-exec
syslog/linux-auditd-anom-login-failures
syslog/linux-auditd-apparmor-denied
